rk with Jeremy  hunts on the future of UK's relationship  with the EU thank you I do not know both  of them personally and there is a golden  rule which I respect that I will work in  a very constructive way with every head  of state or government my other last one  I have a very general question to  conclude maybe a personal one too but  why exactly did you want this job from  the outset given specially given the  challenge that it represented for you  plain and simple because my I I was born  European and my my first as I grew up in  Brussels and as everything was European  at the very beginning and I come from a  family that has a European history it  was I always wanted to be back in the  European environment which was for me of  course then work here even the  Commission ORN and other places and  therefore after having of course made  quite some detours  by training I'm a medical doctor so I've  been working quite a while in the  medical field and I came pretty late  into politics I was beginning of 40s  when I started with politics it was an  enormous gift and I learned tremendously  during those now 13 years with Angela  Merkel in a cabinet but as I said I  always wanted to come back to my roots  and I'm very very happy to be here  Marat Fintan post Italy you talked a lot  of the intention of building a stronger  Europe does this mean that you won't  accept Eric sceptical commissioners  which could be proposed by nationalists  or populist government's around Europe  such as Italy for example thank you I  want a commission that is working to  strengthen Europe to position Europe in  this world in its appropriate role and  Europe is needed in this world and on  this within this difficult and changing  environment and I am deeply convinced  that we all do better when we work  better together all of us no 28 member  states that none of us on its own will  be as successful tackling the problems  as we are together  28 member states and this it's beauty  and the se